**Title:** Top-level `knn` runs a redundant query phase for pure kNN searches (DFS → query → fetch), doubling coordinator fan-out for no observable benefit

### Description

`TransportSearchAction.adjustSearchType` promotes every search containing a top-level `knn` section to `DFS_QUERY_THEN_FETCH`, unconditionally:

```java
static void adjustSearchType(SearchRequest searchRequest, boolean singleShard) {
    // if there's a kNN search, always use DFS_QUERY_THEN_FETCH
    if (searchRequest.hasKnnSearch()) {
        searchRequest.searchType(DFS_QUERY_THEN_FETCH);
        return;
    }
    ...
```

For top-level `knn` the DFS round is the mechanism that implements the **global-k contract** of the top-level `knn` element: every shard runs HNSW and returns its local top-k (`DfsPhase.executeKnnVectorQuery`); the coordinator merges them to exactly k (`DfsQueryPhase`, `TopDocs.merge(k, …)`); then the query phase runs on each shard with the `knn` section replaced by a `KnnScoreDocQueryBuilder` that matches only that shard's survivors (`SearchDfsQueryThenFetchAsyncAction.rewriteShardSearchRequest`). That is what makes the matched set — the thing `hits.total`, aggregations, `post_filter`, `collapse`, field `sort`, and pagination all operate on — exactly k documents rather than k × shards. When a text query is also present, the same round doubles as the BM25 statistics pass for hybrid scoring.

